.TH CHTTP 7 "2026-10-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
chttp \- overview of the HTTP types that chttpclient and chttpserver share
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttp.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
chttp.h holds the parts that the HTTP client and the HTTP server
share.  chttpclient.h and chttpserver.h both include it, so you only
include it yourself when your code needs these types without either
module.  It gives:
  - chttp_method_t, the HTTP methods, and chttp_method_str(3), which
    gives the uppercase name of a method;
  - the CHTTP_STATUS_* status codes (CHTTP_STATUS(3));
  - chttp_tls_config_t, the TLS configuration of both modules, and
    CHTTP_TLS_DEFAULT(3);
  - chttp_request_body_t, the body of a client request, with
    CHTTP_NO_BODY(3), CHTTP_BODY(3), CHTTP_JSON_BODY(3),
    CHTTP_TEXT_BODY(3) and CHTTP_FORM_BODY(3);
  - base64 helpers (chttp_base64_encode_mp(3),
    chttp_base64_decode_mp(3)) and the RFC 7617 Basic auth helper
    chttp_basic_auth_mp(3).
.fi
.SS "Methods"
.nf
chttp_method_t names the seven methods GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH,
HEAD and OPTIONS, plus CHTTP_ANY. CHTTP_ANY is a wildcard for server
routes and matches any of the seven. It is never the method of a real
request, and chttp_request_create_mp(3) refuses it for a client
request.
.fi
.SS "TLS configuration"
.nf
A chttp_tls_config_t whose bytes are all zero is the strict
configuration, and CHTTP_TLS_DEFAULT(3) is that same value with a
name. Every field that weakens a check is named for what it gives up
(insecure_skip_verify, insecure_skip_hostname_check,
client_cert_optional), so a struct that you fill only partly never
verifies less than you asked for. On a client, the defaults verify the
server's certificate chain against ca_bundle_path or the system trust
store, and match the host of the request against that certificate. On
a server, a ca_bundle_path requires every client to present a
certificate that verifies against it, unless client_cert_optional is
set. Each module ignores the fields that only the other one uses.

cert_path, key_path and ca_bundle_path must each name a regular file of
at most 16 MB. A ca_bundle_path must hold at least one CA certificate.
When it also holds a certificate revocation list, every certificate of
the peer's chain below its trust anchor needs a CRL of its issuer in
the bundle, and that CRL must not be past its next update time.
.fi
.SS "Ownership"
.nf
A chttp_request_body_t does not own its bytes. chttp_request_create_mp(3)
copies them, so you can free the source as soon as that call returns.

chttp_base64_encode_mp(3), chttp_base64_decode_mp(3) and
chttp_basic_auth_mp(3) return a new buffer that the caller owns. Free
it with the free function of the allocator you passed, or with free(3)
if you passed NULL. The forms without _mp use malloc(3). All of them
return NULL when the allocation fails or the input is not valid.
.fi
.SS "Strict input"
.nf
The decoder refuses malformed input and never tries to repair it. That
covers an invalid character, padding in the wrong place, nonzero
padding bits and a length that is not a multiple of 4. The decoded
bytes can contain NUL bytes, so take their length from out_len and
never from strlen(3).
chttp_basic_auth_mp(3) refuses a user name with a colon in it, because
the receiver splits the decoded text at its first colon. It also
refuses a user name or a password with a control character in it,
which RFC 7617 forbids in both.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
The functions of chttp.h work only on their arguments and keep no
state, so any thread can call any of them at any time.
.fi
